WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.332
One attention pattern is usually not enough to model all useful relationships in language.

00:00:05.332 --> 00:00:09.592
Multi-head attention creates several parallel attention heads.

00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:14.097
Each head has its own learned Q, K, and V projections,

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:17.737
so each one operates in a different learned subspace.

00:00:17.737 --> 00:00:22.172
The heads can therefore develop different patterns for combining information.

00:00:22.172 --> 00:00:28.407
We should avoid saying that head one always learns grammar and head two always learns entities;

00:00:28.407 --> 00:00:33.042
real behavior is more complicated and varies by layer and model.

00:00:33.042 --> 00:00:35.472
The useful idea is diversity.

00:00:35.472 --> 00:00:39.187
Instead of forcing every relationship through one attention map,

00:00:39.187 --> 00:00:43.022
the model can represent several interaction patterns at once.

00:00:43.022 --> 00:00:50.087
After each head produces its context vectors, the outputs are concatenated and passed through another linear

00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:53.757
projection so the combined result returns to the model’s hidden size.

00:00:53.757 --> 00:00:58.712
In PyTorch, num_heads controls how many parallel heads are created,

00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:01.619
and embed_dim must be compatible with that split.
